+### Named tests
+
+A task may name the test cases that prove it in `tests:`, a block list (a flow list is refused by rule 16). The plan's `test_command`, `test_pass_prefix` and `test_fail_prefix` say how a test file is run and how its output is read.
+
+**Entry grammar.** The raw entry is taken literally, with no YAML unquoting inside it. It is split at the first ` :: `. `<file>` is trimmed and a leading `./` is removed. `<case>` is trimmed. Two entries are the same entry when their normalised `<file>`, `<case>` pairs are equal. `<file>` must be one of the task's `files:`. A case name must be unique in its file: the matcher cannot tell two cases with one name apart, so such a name reads as ambiguous.
+
+**Matching.** `verify-tests` runs `test_command` with every `{file}` replaced by the single-quoted test file, as `sh -c` from the repository root. A line of the output that is exactly `test_pass_prefix` followed by the case name is a pass line. A line that is `test_fail_prefix` followed by the case name, either alone or followed by ` (` and detail, is a fail line. Each case then comes out as one of:
+
+- passed: one pass line and no fail line.
+- failed: one fail line and no pass line.
+- absent: no pass line and no fail line.
+- ambiguous: any other mix, such as both a pass and a fail line, or the same line twice.
+
+**`base_sha`.** `plan-state set <plan> <id> running` writes `base_sha:` (the commit the working tree was at) the first time the task goes to `running`, and only then. A resume of a `running` task keeps the value, so the baseline stays the commit before the task's first dispatch. Nobody edits it by hand.
+
+**Labels.** `verify-tests` labels each named case, with the run at `base_sha` as the baseline and the run at the task's result as the outcome. A case gets the first label that applies, in this order, so each case has exactly one label:
+
+1. `ambiguous`: the case is ambiguous in either run.
+2. `not-green`: not passed at the result.
+3. `not-red`: passed at the baseline. The case proves nothing new.
+4. `ok-absent`: absent at the baseline, passed at the result. The test file or case is new.
+5. `ok`: failed at the baseline, passed at the result. The test went red, then green.
+
+`ok` and `ok-absent` count as ok. `ambiguous`, `not-green` and `not-red` count as failed, in the total and for the exit code.
